Context (from this page and its neighbours): Ĝ⁺ is a profinite group with distinguished elements ρ, σ and ε₀, and Ĝ^♮ ⊂ Ĝ⁺ is a distinguished subgroup. The 2/3 subscripts suggest, though the page does not confirm it, that ρ and σ are the elliptic elements of orders 2 and 3 of the profinite modular group (Z/2 * Z/3)^ and ε₀ a parabolic element. Here μ = {±1}. The group Z_{ρ,σ} consists of quadruples (u, χ, ε₂, ε₃) with u ∈ Aut(Ĝ⁺), χ ∈ Ẑ*, ε₂, ε₃ ∈ μ. Its conditions are: u sends ρ and σ to Ĝ^♮-conjugates of their 'ε-twisted' versions (twisting by an orientation-reversing τ), and u sends ε₀ to a conjugate of ε₀^χ. It comes with a homomorphism Z → Aut(Ĝ⁺). L₀^♮ is a group of 'inner' elements (presumably taken inside Ĝ^♮) embedded in Z by i_{L₀,Z} and in Ĝ^♮ by i_{L₀,Ĝ}. The quotient γ_{ρ,σ} = Z/L₀^♮ is the 'outer' group carrying the characters χ_γ, ε_{2γ}, ε_{3γ}. Page 658 forms the semidirect products SoG = Z⋉Ĝ⁺ ⊃ SoG^♮ = Z⋉Ĝ^♮. It also gives the antidiagonal homomorphism L₀^♮ → SoG^♮, l ↦ i_{L₀,Z}(l)·l⁻¹.

On this page Grothendieck notes that this image is normal ('d'image invariante'). He then defines (29) G_{ρ,σ} := SoG/Im L₀^♮ and G^♮_{ρ,σ} := SoG^♮/Im L₀^♮. The under-bar annotation is not a fraction: it records SoG = Z_{ρ,σ}·Ĝ⁺. This gives the exact sequences (30) 1→L₀^♮→SoG^{(♮)}→G^{(♮)}→1. The commutative diagram (31) contains L₀^♮→Z→G^♮ and L₀^♮→Ĝ^♮→G^♮, Ĝ^♮⊂Ĝ⁺→G, and G^♮↪G. The diagram (32) of extensions reads:
- top row: 1→L₀^♮→Z→γ→1;
- middle row: its pushout along L₀^♮→Ĝ^♮, namely 1→Ĝ^♮→G^♮→γ→1;
- bottom row: 1→Ĝ⁺→G→γ→1, with the same quotient γ.

In modern terms this is the standard 'pushout of an extension along a G-equivariant map' / extension realizing a given outer action. It is the device for turning a group of outer automorphisms preserving conjugacy-class data (a GT-like group γ_{ρ,σ}) into a genuine group G_{ρ,σ} containing Ĝ⁺ as a normal subgroup. In (33) the characters of γ are pulled back to G via δ_G: χ_G, ε_{2G}, ε_{3G}. In (34) he defines the kernel subgroups G', G'', G''', G° (as for γ in (24)) and G⁺ = Ker χ_G with its primed variants, γ⁺ = Ker χ_γ, and the same for G^♮ (margin note 'item avec G^♮'). In (35) τ_G ∈ G^♮ ⊂ G and τ_γ ∈ γ are named; the next page identifies them as images of the element τ_Z of (26). There Ĝ is recovered as the inverse image of {1, τ_γ}, i.e. G_{ρ,σ} is an enlargement of the 'orientation double' Ĝ of Ĝ⁺. Purpose: to build an arithmetic-Teichmüller-type automorphism group of Ĝ⁺ (an analogue of GT adapted to the (2,3,∞)/modular setting with signs ε₂, ε₃) as an extension, and then (page 633) to describe it via Φ: G^♮ → Aut(Ĝ⁺)×Ẑ*×μ×μ.
